89
00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:36.090
To me, that was superstar status that he wrote that just amazing satire.

90
00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:38.510
It makes me want to go back and rewatch it.

91
00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:42.530
I had forgotten that he worked on it, and when I was looking up his credits, I was like,

92
00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:44.170
oh, I want to rewatch The Comeback.

93
00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:48.390
Well, fast fact number three, Mike Schur wrote a book.

94
00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:53.310
It's called How to Be Perfect, The Correct Answer to Every Moral Question.

95
00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:56.450
Here is how he described it to us in an email.

96
00:03:57.170 --> 00:04:02.870
He said, it's a book about how hard it is to be a good person and how we can use ethics

97
00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:07.750
and moral philosophy to try not to screw up all the time, but in a funny way, not like

98
00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:08.730
in a homework way.

99
00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:13.350
The basic idea was to explain all the stuff I learned for The Good Place without giving

100
00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:14.330
everyone a headache.

101
00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:19.690
You guys, when we did this interview with Mike, our copies of his book had not arrived

102
00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:24.750
yet, but we have them now, and this is a truly special book.

103
00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:27.990
I started reading it, and I can't stop.

104
00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:28.310
Same.

105
00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:29.350
I'm like binging a book.

106
00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:29.830
Same.

107
00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:30.870
I love it.

108
00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:31.810
I couldn't put it down.

109
00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:33.050
I was reading it last night.

110
00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:34.550
This morning, I was like, I want to read more.

111
00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:36.230
Like, I woke up thinking about it.

112
00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:36.910
Do you know what I mean?

113
00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:37.190
Yeah.

114
00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:39.470
So Mike is such an amazing writer.

115
00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:40.350
We already know that.

116
00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:46.150
But he takes this conversation of morality, and he makes it funny and interesting and

117
00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:46.710
relatable.

118
00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:51.770
For example, there's a chapter titled, and by the way, all the chapter titles are hilarious,

119
00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:53.530
but here's one that cracked me up.

120
00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:58.830
There's a chapter titled, Do I Have to Return My Shopping Cart to the Shopping Cart Rack Thingy?

121
00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:00.930
It's all the way over there.

122
00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:01.910
Yes.

123
00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:09.470
And then basically what he does is he answers that question, and the answer is based on all

124
00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:13.030
of these ancient philosophers and, you know.

125
00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:15.830
Ethics and morality and your moral compass.

126
00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:16.590
Yes.

127
00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:22.790
So listen, I think we should take a break, and when we come back, you get to listen to Mike

128
00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:23.090
Schur.

129
00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:26.170
And we get to find out so much about Moe's.

130
00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:27.910
It's a treat, we promise.

131
00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:39.160
Schur, welcome to Office Ladies.

132
00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:39.660
Woo-hoo!

133
00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:41.400
I'm so excited to be here.

134
00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:46.320
I'm a huge fan of you as people, first of all, first and foremoSt.

135
00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:48.580
Second of all, you as actors.

136
00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:50.740
And then third of all, you as podcasters.

137
00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:55.600
This has been such a delight to watch you do this thing to celebrate this show that

138
00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:56.280
we all made together.

139
00:05:56.280 --> 00:05:57.520
I'm so happy to be here.

140
00:05:57.580 --> 00:05:58.460
It's a real honor.

141
00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:00.340
Oh, Mike, thank you so much.

142
00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:01.140
We love you.

143
00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:05.300
We're so thrilled to have you here, and I know the fans are going to enjoy just hearing

144
00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:06.300
your voice.

145
00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:10.900
Jen and I both listened to you quite a bit before this, and we both found you really

146
00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:11.860
pleasing to listen to.

147
00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:12.540
Really?

148
00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:14.000
Yeah, we like your voice quite a lot.

149
00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:14.440
We like your voice.

150
00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:16.560
You listen to me in what context?

151
00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:17.920
Other podcasts, Mike.

152
00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:18.440
Where was I talking?

153
00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:19.100
Interviews, podcasts.

154
00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:21.300
We do our research here on Office Ladies.

155
00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:21.720
Yeah.

156
00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:25.880
That's very nice of you to say, because I've always found my own voice to be slightly

157
00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:28.140
annoying, which I think maybe most people do.

158
00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:32.960
You're like, it's the phenomenon of hearing your voice played back to you in this is how

159
00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:36.280
old we are, is like on an answering machine message or something.

160
00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:38.920
If you heard, you know, you'd be like, oh, is that what I sound like?

161
00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:42.960
I always feel like I sound a little annoying and nasally, but I'm glad to hear you say that.

162
00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:47.500
Well, I felt that way about myself too, and I sort of brushed it off like, oh, that's

163
00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:49.820
just everybody feels that way about their own voice.

164
00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:56.120
But then we recently rewatched an episode where Michael is trying to get Jim to say something

165
00:06:56.120 --> 00:07:01.400
bad about Pam, and he gets Jim to admit that Pam's voice is rather shrill sometimes.

166
00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:08.760
And I thought, oh, I guess maybe in the writer's room, when forced to come up with something

167
00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:12.160
annoying about me, we settled on my shrill voice.

168
00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:13.060
So thank you so much.

169
00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:14.960
That didn't give me a complex or anything.

170
00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:16.080
Which writer hated your voice?

171
00:07:16.120 --> 00:07:16.840
I don't know.

172
00:07:17.140 --> 00:07:18.040
Mike, do you know?

173
00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:21.760
I'm very, very pleased to say that I think I was gone by the time that was written,

174
00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:23.800
so I do not have to answer for that crime.

175
00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:24.560
Well played.

176
00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:25.200
No.

177
00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:25.660
It wasn't you.

178
00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:30.820
Well, listen, we always like to ask people when they come on the podcast, how did they

179
00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:32.900
get their job on the office?

180
00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:35.060
And we know that you did double duty.

181
00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:38.140
You were a writer, but then you were also a performer.

182
00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:41.560
But let's start with how did you get your job as a writer on the show?

183
00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:47.180
So I was writing at Saturday Night Live from 1998 to 2004.

184
00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:52.020
My then girlfriend, now wife of 16 years, had moved out to L.A.

185
00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:53.900
permanently in like 2002,

186
00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:55.300
and we were dating long distance.

187
00:07:56.100 --> 00:08:01.740
And in like 2003, it was like, all right, if this is ever going to really work, either

188
00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:05.140
she had to move back to New York or I had to leave and move to L.A., and it made more

189
00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:06.180
sense for me to move to L.A.

190
00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:07.880
because there are more jobs there.

191
00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:14.480
So I gave my like year's notice to Lauren Michaels and said I was leaving at the end

192
00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:21.000
of the year and got an agent for the first time in my life because I never needed one.

193
00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:22.300
And I was like, all right, I'm moving.

194
00:08:22.780 --> 00:08:28.380
And the first thing he told me was, you know, this guy, Greg Daniels, is adapting the British

195
00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:30.140
office for American television.

196
00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:33.920
And I responded, what a terrible idea.

197
00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:35.580
It's just a disaster.

198
00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:37.160
You shouldn't do this.

199
00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:39.640
No one should do this because I was a huge fan of the British show.

200
00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:41.500
This is such a classic story, right?

201
00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:43.680
I was a huge fan of the British show.

202
00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:45.300
I thought it was absolutely brilliant.

203
00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:47.700
I didn't think there was any way it would ever work in America.

204
00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:53.500
But it was like, well, you know, beggars can't be choosers like I have to get a job out there.

205
00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:55.260
So I came to L.A.

206
00:08:56.020 --> 00:09:00.280
sometime in I don't even remember, like like winter, I think like February

207
00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:02.060
on an off week at SNL.

208
00:09:02.180 --> 00:09:04.560
And I had a bunch of meetings and one of them was with Greg.

209
00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:10.720
So most of the meetings I had with producers were pretty standard hour long chats.

210
00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:14.120
It's, you know, hey, here's our here's our pilot.

211
00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:16.040
Watch the pilot and let's talk about the show.

212
00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:20.740
And I dutifully tried to, you know, say nice things about whatever they were working on.

213
00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:26.260
And then I met with Greg and my meeting with Greg was two hours long, more than two hours

214
00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:26.560
long.

215
00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:30.200
And it was so different from every other meeting.

216
00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:36.780
It was it was like intense and in-depth and fascinating.

217
00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:41.940
In the middle of the meeting, he said, I'm very sorry, but my back is acting up.

218
00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:43.520
Would you mind if I lied down?

219
00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:45.140
And I said, no.

220
00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:50.480
And so he lay on the floor on his back in front of me while I sat on his couch.

221
00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:56.360
So I think to an outside observer, it would look very much like we were in like Freudian

222
00:09:56.360 --> 00:09:59.880
analysis where I was his therapist and he was like free associating.

223
00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:06.800
But he just asked the most incisive questions, the most penetrating questions about the nature

224
00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:10.640
of storytelling and about the British show and why it worked and what he had to do to

225
00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:12.440
make it work here.

226
00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:18.280
And I left the meeting and I texted on my like Motorola Razr.

227
00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:24.820
I texted my agent and I said, I don't know if that guy's going to offer me a job, but if

228
00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:27.640
he does, I'm going to take it because I think he's going to teach me how to write.

229
00:10:29.260 --> 00:10:34.680
And my agent sends me that text like every four years back to me.

230
00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:36.540
He sends it back to me and says like, you were right.

231
00:10:36.620 --> 00:10:36.960
You were right.

232
00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:37.280
You're right.

233
00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:44.640
Um, so, so Greg offered me the job, um, and I happily took it and I thought this is obviously

234
00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:45.300
not going to work.

235
00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:51.820
Uh, it's a, it is a, um, it is a fool's errand to try to adapt this show for an American

236
00:10:51.820 --> 00:10:54.160
audience, but he's going to teach me how to write.

237
00:10:54.340 --> 00:10:56.320
And that's exactly what happened in that first season.

238
00:10:56.320 --> 00:11:02.720
And, you know, the only full-time writers really were me, Mindy and BJ because, um, Lester

239
00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:07.920
Lewis, the late Lester Lewis and Paul Eberstein and Larry Wilmore were consultants and Mindy

240
00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:09.220
and BJ and I had never written anything.

241
00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:10.840
We didn't have any idea what we were doing.

242
00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:13.060
And Greg basically taught a class.

243
00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:15.880
He taught a, uh, like the true professor he is.

244
00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:20.900
He taught a class on how to write and, um, and it changed all of our lives forever.

245
00:11:20.900 --> 00:11:23.280
So that, that's my office origin story.

246
00:11:23.660 --> 00:11:24.100
Wow.

247
00:11:24.220 --> 00:11:25.180
That is so good.

248
00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:31.760
Mike, I remember you guys, um, that first season, there were so few of you and, um,

249
00:11:32.060 --> 00:11:34.540
how close you all became because of that.

250
00:11:35.460 --> 00:11:35.640
Yeah.

251
00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:39.720
It was a tiny little, Greg used to call it, uh, a strike force.

252
00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:44.000
He was like, this is an elite strike force, which I think was just him trying to boost

253
00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:46.540
our confidence because we had no idea what we were doing.

254
00:11:46.540 --> 00:11:52.120
Um, but it was really, you know, it was, it was a very, very small group of people who

255
00:11:52.120 --> 00:11:56.500
were working really, really hard on a very small number of episodes.

256
00:11:56.540 --> 00:11:58.400
We only had, we only wrote five scripts.

257
00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:06.100
Like, um, you know, we each wrote, we, we actually wrote, uh, we wrote six to make five.

258
00:12:06.280 --> 00:12:10.280
I'm not sure if you remember this, but we wrote, or even if you even knew this, Greg,

259
00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:14.260
one of the only things that Greg was able to squeeze out of NBC in that first year was

260
00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:17.860
he let us write one more script than we would shoot.

261
00:12:18.140 --> 00:12:22.760
And so we wrote one extra episode, um, that I think he wrote.

262
00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:27.620
And then we just, we picked the best five out of the six that we had written and threw

263
00:12:27.620 --> 00:12:28.320
the other one away.

264
00:12:28.460 --> 00:12:30.340
And I wish I could remember what that other one was.

265
00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:31.480
There is a, there is a loSt.

266
00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:33.580
Oh, that'd be a good question for Greg.

267
00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:34.060
Yeah.

268
00:12:34.120 --> 00:12:38.320
There's a lost season one office episode script out there somewhere that we just

269
00:12:38.320 --> 00:12:40.440
never, uh, that we never produced.

270
00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:46.400
Um, so it was, and it was like, you know, it was really intense work and it was a combination

271
00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:53.040
of, of like story breaking, but also just like a masterclass in how to create television

272
00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:54.340
and what makes a good story.

273
00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:58.000
There were times when I've told the story before, but there are times when Greg was talking,

274
00:12:58.640 --> 00:13:05.460
we would be pitching and I had a notebook where I would like just jot down ideas and there

275
00:13:05.460 --> 00:13:09.480
would be moments when Greg would say, um, well, let's talk about what, let's just pause

276
00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:12.740
for a second and talk about like what makes a good story.

277
00:13:12.980 --> 00:13:17.240
And then he would start kind of like going through the list of things that he felt were

278
00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:18.800
crucial to good TV stories.

279
00:13:19.060 --> 00:13:24.180
And I would realize like, oh, he's in lecture mode is what the way I thought of it was lecture

280
00:13:24.180 --> 00:13:25.620
mode, which was a good thing.

281
00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:27.620
It sounds like a bad thing, but it was a good thing.

282
00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:30.920
And I would turn the page and I would start taking notes like I was in college.

283
00:13:30.920 --> 00:13:35.680
And I would just like write down as if he were an actual professor and I had a test coming

284
00:13:35.680 --> 00:13:39.500
up and I would start just writing down and I still have those notes and I still go back

285
00:13:39.500 --> 00:13:43.380
and look at them every once in a while to like remember, to refresh my memory about what

286
00:13:43.380 --> 00:13:44.780
he told us all about storytelling.

287
00:13:45.500 --> 00:13:50.300
It was that, that intense and that sort of foundational and meaningful to all of us.

288
00:13:50.500 --> 00:13:51.100
Oh man.

289
00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:56.180
I do remember those early days about what a small collective we were.

290
00:13:56.440 --> 00:13:57.920
Even our table reads, do you remember?

291
00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:01.340
We would just pull up chairs on the stage where we were filming.

292
00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:06.500
We would just all grab like a desk chair and sit in a circle and read the script.

293
00:14:06.660 --> 00:14:08.360
And I loved that.

294
00:14:09.120 --> 00:14:10.420
It was really intimate.

295
00:14:10.860 --> 00:14:18.420
There were so many cool things about that first season, including that the place where

296
00:14:18.420 --> 00:14:22.900
we worked was on this weird soundstage in the middle of Culver City or something.

297
00:14:22.900 --> 00:14:29.220
And so Hollywood soundstages are often, you know, these big gigantic empty rooms where

298
00:14:29.220 --> 00:14:34.660
you put sets and then attached to them usually or above them are a suite of offices where the

299
00:14:34.660 --> 00:14:36.300
writers and the production staff works.

300
00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:41.600
But that suite of offices became the actual set.

301
00:14:41.800 --> 00:14:46.480
Like the set that we built was modeled on the crappy offices where the writers were working.

302
00:14:46.480 --> 00:14:51.300
And right down to the layout, like Greg was in what would be Michael's office.

303
00:14:51.500 --> 00:14:55.240
And there was like a reception desk right outside it that became Pam's reception desk.

304
00:14:55.440 --> 00:14:57.300
And so we would do these drills.

305
00:14:57.640 --> 00:15:02.260
Greg would have us do these drills where he would go like, okay, take a half an hour and

306
00:15:02.260 --> 00:15:07.660
like go sit at Pam's desk or go sit in Michael's office or what will be Michael's office.

307
00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:14.360
Or go, go sit where Jim sits and like, just get a sense of like, like, what is your eyeline

308
00:15:14.360 --> 00:15:15.600
to Pam, right?

309
00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:20.720
So he would put, he would put Mindy at Pam's reception desk and he would put BJ at Jim's

310
00:15:20.720 --> 00:15:21.240
desk.

311
00:15:21.380 --> 00:15:26.060
And he would say like, just sit there and like absorb the vibe of like, imagine that you're

312
00:15:26.060 --> 00:15:30.900
in love with that person and that you have to like angle your chair in such a way so that

313
00:15:30.900 --> 00:15:33.400
you can catch a glimpse of her out of the corner of your eye or whatever.

314
00:15:33.880 --> 00:15:35.380
And it was fascinating.

315
00:15:35.380 --> 00:15:36.260
It was really interesting.

316
00:15:36.260 --> 00:15:40.600
And I remember at one point he had me go into work in Michael's office or what the office

317
00:15:40.600 --> 00:15:42.320
that would be the model for Michael's office.

318
00:15:42.540 --> 00:15:47.340
And I remember hating it because it was like, I was in this, I was far away from everything

319
00:15:47.340 --> 00:15:50.760
and all this, and I would see people talking through the windows and I'd be like, what

320
00:15:50.760 --> 00:15:51.520
are they talking about?

321
00:15:51.720 --> 00:15:56.140
And I had the urge to like go out and like do what Michael did all the time, which was

322
00:15:56.140 --> 00:15:57.320
like, Hey, what's going on?

323
00:15:57.440 --> 00:16:03.360
You know, like, and it was like, he was just so, um, he knew so much about what he wanted

324
00:16:03.360 --> 00:16:09.020
out of the show that he would, he would practice not just the skill of writing the characters,

325
00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:13.020
but the, the skill of like embodying them or emotionally connecting to them.

326
00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:17.280
And it was just, that's what I mean when I say it was professorial, it was, it wasn't

327
00:16:17.280 --> 00:16:19.540
just like, all right, what's episode two?

328
00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:20.660
What's the act break?

329
00:16:20.840 --> 00:16:24.060
What's the first act break for the, for the season finale?

330
00:16:24.060 --> 00:16:30.780
It was really like, we had to embody the characters and understand them emotionally and, and, and

331
00:16:30.780 --> 00:16:35.020
socially in a way that I think, uh, led to great episodes, you know?

332
00:16:35.340 --> 00:16:39.380
Well, it's so interesting because when all the actors got there, then they did that to

333
00:16:39.380 --> 00:16:39.840
us too.

334
00:16:39.960 --> 00:16:43.400
They made us sit at our desks and just sit there and feel it.

335
00:16:43.620 --> 00:16:45.240
We've talked about that before.

336
00:16:45.240 --> 00:16:50.540
And so I love that that started all the way back with you guys writing the episodes.

337
00:16:50.540 --> 00:16:56.320
Yeah, June of June of 2004, like they're right from the, from the jump, there were things

338
00:16:56.320 --> 00:17:01.560
like that, that we did that weren't, um, that weren't the typical things that you would do

339
00:17:01.560 --> 00:17:02.360
with a writing staff.

340
00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:06.780
Another thing he did all the time, which I thought was so smart, which I still do on shows that

341
00:17:06.780 --> 00:17:11.560
I work on now is we would come into work one day and he would go, he would have, he would

342
00:17:11.560 --> 00:17:15.380
put all the characters names on a board and he would pick two of them at random and he

343
00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:21.600
would give them to, he would say me and Larry Wilmore, like go come up with five story ideas

344
00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:23.400
for Oscar and Kelly.

345
00:17:24.060 --> 00:17:30.900
And then, and then you, Mindy and Paul, like go come up with five story ideas for, uh, you

346
00:17:30.900 --> 00:17:32.400
know, Jim and Stanley.

347
00:17:33.760 --> 00:17:39.180
And it, for an hour, we would just pitch, okay, well, what the hell could a story be with Oscar

348
00:17:39.180 --> 00:17:39.720
and Kelly?

349
00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:44.060
But it, it led to some really cool stuff and like some of those, some of the little, the

350
00:17:44.060 --> 00:17:49.300
B stories and the C stories of, of that, that came that were in the first couple of seasons

351
00:17:49.300 --> 00:17:53.260
were just brainstorming like random pairings of people.

352
00:17:53.600 --> 00:17:59.140
He, he understood fundamentally that one of the great advantages that the show had over

353
00:17:59.140 --> 00:18:01.420
other shows is that we had 20 people.

354
00:18:01.420 --> 00:18:06.360
We had like most shows have whatever, six main characters or something.

355
00:18:06.680 --> 00:18:13.580
And so pretty quickly you get tired of a story with involving these two people or these three

356
00:18:13.580 --> 00:18:13.980
people.

357
00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:16.580
And he was like, that's not what this is going to be.

358
00:18:16.700 --> 00:18:21.100
This is going to be a, the whole point of the show is to observe like an anthropologist,

359
00:18:21.320 --> 00:18:26.580
observe the comings and goings of a large number of people in, in the same office environment.

360
00:18:26.580 --> 00:18:32.320
So like go work on random pairings and like sometimes really fun stuff would come out of

361
00:18:32.320 --> 00:18:32.460
it.

362
00:18:32.560 --> 00:18:39.000
You know, like he just so thoroughly understood what, what made the show special and what the

363
00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:44.240
potential was for how good it could be that he had all of these machinations and he was

364
00:18:44.240 --> 00:18:48.240
moved, always moving pieces around the chessboard with the writers in those for, in those early

365
00:18:48.240 --> 00:18:48.780
days, you know?

366
00:18:49.260 --> 00:18:49.580
Yeah.

367
00:18:49.760 --> 00:18:54.680
I, I remember telling my dad, I was trying to describe this group of people and the show

368
00:18:54.680 --> 00:18:58.940
to my dad who is, was not at all in entertainment, you know, he was a drilling engineer.

369
00:18:59.640 --> 00:19:03.480
But sometimes we could talk sports and I said, dad, here's the deal.

370
00:19:03.620 --> 00:19:05.480
We have a deep bench, dad.

371
00:19:05.940 --> 00:19:07.460
And it was like a basketball term.

372
00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:08.740
Like our team is so good.

373
00:19:09.040 --> 00:19:10.740
There's so many layers to this team.

374
00:19:11.240 --> 00:19:16.380
And, and then when he came and, you know, Greg invited them to sit at a table read at lunch.

375
00:19:16.880 --> 00:19:18.920
My dad was like, I get it.

376
00:19:19.040 --> 00:19:19.580
I get it.

377
00:19:19.660 --> 00:19:19.800
I get it now.

378
00:19:19.920 --> 00:19:20.020
Yeah.

379
00:19:20.180 --> 00:19:20.400
I get it.

380
00:19:20.460 --> 00:19:24.540
Like, cause there were laughs, I think coming from him and all these places, you know?

381
00:19:25.120 --> 00:19:25.400
Yeah.

382
00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:31.700
Well, I was going to say that, um, in season nine, John and I were invited to be producers

383
00:19:31.700 --> 00:19:35.940
on the show to kind of help carve out Jim and Pam's final season.

384
00:19:35.940 --> 00:19:40.620
And we got to come into their writer's room, mostly as observers.

385
00:19:41.080 --> 00:19:46.320
And Greg was running the show that season and he was still doing those exercises.

386
00:19:46.320 --> 00:19:52.260
And it was fascinating, except in this case, it was more like homework.

387
00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:57.940
I remember, um, the day started and Greg said, okay, get out your homework.

388
00:19:58.220 --> 00:20:05.020
And the homework had been that everyone had to come up with five ideas for, I can't remember

389
00:20:05.020 --> 00:20:09.360
what it was, but it was like for a Dwight and Stanley cold open, go.

390
00:20:10.760 --> 00:20:13.680
And, um, and then you would just sit and listen.

391
00:20:14.300 --> 00:20:18.180
And then when there was an idea that piqued his interest, he would say, oh, oh, okay.

392
00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:19.880
You two go off, flesh that out.

393
00:20:19.880 --> 00:20:24.140
And then two people would leave the room and then we would keep listening to the homework.

394
00:20:24.360 --> 00:20:31.260
And I, I remember thinking, oh my gosh, I would be so terrified to present my five ideas

395
00:20:31.260 --> 00:20:33.220
because sometimes it was just crickets.

396
00:20:33.220 --> 00:20:36.780
Like you could just hear the ideas just die in the room.

397
00:20:37.420 --> 00:20:45.820
Um, it, I mean, what an intense and vulnerable thing to pitch your creative ideas in front

398
00:20:45.820 --> 00:20:49.780
of like a room of people who are all judging whether or not you should follow that thread.

399
00:20:50.260 --> 00:20:50.780
Yes.

400
00:20:50.920 --> 00:20:56.920
One of his greatest strengths is that he is unafraid, I would say, of failure.

401
00:20:57.360 --> 00:21:03.140
Um, and I think, I believe that that comes, I'm guessing that that comes partially from working

402
00:21:03.140 --> 00:21:09.240
at Saturday night live, because what you just described of like pitching ideas and, and having

403
00:21:09.240 --> 00:21:10.120
them bomb, right?

404
00:21:10.320 --> 00:21:15.960
That is the essence of working at SNL, except your failures at SNL are public.

405
00:21:16.160 --> 00:21:21.820
Like you write sketches and there's a read through with the entire cast of the, of the show and

406
00:21:21.820 --> 00:21:24.660
Lauren and all the producers around a giant table.

407
00:21:24.800 --> 00:21:29.780
And the audience is a hundred and 150 people who work on the show.

408
00:21:29.780 --> 00:21:35.480
So, and by the way, a very famous person who's hosting the show, like, you know, Drake is

409
00:21:35.480 --> 00:21:37.400
reading your, your sketch or whatever.

410
00:21:38.020 --> 00:21:43.740
And you, there are moments at SNL where, you know, your sketches are 12 pages long or whatever.

411
00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:48.100
And you're, the sketch starts and you get to page two and like the joke of the sketch

412
00:21:48.100 --> 00:21:49.840
is sprung and no one laughs.

413
00:21:50.160 --> 00:21:52.760
And in your head, you're like, there's 10 more pages.

414
00:21:53.120 --> 00:21:57.140
Like we have to sit here for 10 more pages as no one finds my premise funny.

415
00:21:57.140 --> 00:22:00.780
And so for the first, I don't know what Greg's experience was.

416
00:22:00.900 --> 00:22:05.620
I've never really talked to him about his time there, but for the first like six months,

417
00:22:06.100 --> 00:22:09.340
you, it's, uh, you fall into a deep depression.

418
00:22:09.420 --> 00:22:13.960
Like it's just like, it's so embarrassing to fail that way publicly.

419
00:22:14.720 --> 00:22:19.340
And then the week goes by and the show happens and then it's like, all right, it's Monday

420
00:22:19.340 --> 00:22:20.100
again, do it again.

421
00:22:20.320 --> 00:22:25.000
And you like do it again and you fail again and you fail and you fail and you fail.

422
00:22:25.000 --> 00:22:30.080
And eventually at some point, hopefully you kind of figure it out and you write a sketch

423
00:22:30.080 --> 00:22:33.160
and it gets some laughs and you're like, okay, it's not the end of the world.

424
00:22:33.280 --> 00:22:34.060
Like I'm still here.

425
00:22:34.240 --> 00:22:36.280
And then you get a little better and a little better.

426
00:22:36.440 --> 00:22:41.720
And that, like that theory is, it's pretty brutal on your ego.

427
00:22:42.260 --> 00:22:47.880
But when you've already had it driven out of your ego from working at SNL, like five,

428
00:22:47.880 --> 00:22:50.120
five Stanley cold opens, I can, oh, who cares?

429
00:22:50.120 --> 00:22:50.620
No problem.

430
00:22:50.920 --> 00:22:51.660
Yeah, exactly.

431
00:22:52.180 --> 00:22:56.540
So I, that, and his theory is really like, this is a numbers game.

432
00:22:56.700 --> 00:23:01.280
Like the way to get to the best ideas is you have to create a thousand ideas in order to

433
00:23:01.280 --> 00:23:02.500
find the two that are good.

434
00:23:02.640 --> 00:23:04.960
And he took that approach with everything.

435
00:23:05.080 --> 00:23:06.740
He took that approach when he was hiring writers.

436
00:23:06.900 --> 00:23:08.580
He took that approach when he was casting people.

437
00:23:08.660 --> 00:23:12.960
I mean, how long did the casting process go on, um, for, for the show?

438
00:23:13.480 --> 00:23:15.680
Endlessly long, endlessly, endlessly long.

439
00:23:15.680 --> 00:23:21.300
And that's just his, um, he, he's just the most thorough person when it comes to that.

440
00:23:21.360 --> 00:23:22.660
He doesn't settle for anything.

441
00:23:22.840 --> 00:23:25.260
He, he demands that you walk down.

442
00:23:25.900 --> 00:23:31.700
I mean, when we're, when we're, when we were breaking stories, like we would get five,

443
00:23:31.880 --> 00:23:35.040
sixth of the way down the line toward breaking a story.

444
00:23:35.280 --> 00:23:37.260
And we'd get to a point where he would realize it didn't work.

445
00:23:37.380 --> 00:23:38.520
And he would go like, man, this doesn't work.

446
00:23:38.600 --> 00:23:38.980
Throw it away.

447
00:23:39.140 --> 00:23:39.500
Start over.

448
00:23:39.500 --> 00:23:46.700
And it's really exhausting, but also the show lasted for 10 years and it was really good.

449
00:23:46.900 --> 00:23:47.200
So, um.

450
00:23:47.400 --> 00:23:49.460
And here we are still talking about it.

451
00:23:49.600 --> 00:23:50.460
Yeah, exactly.

452
00:23:50.680 --> 00:23:50.820
Yeah.

453
00:23:51.060 --> 00:23:51.960
17 years later.

454
00:23:52.280 --> 00:23:52.680
Wow.

455
00:24:02.570 --> 00:24:05.070
Well, now I think we have to ask you, Mike.

456
00:24:05.310 --> 00:24:06.070
Yeah, we do.

457
00:24:06.610 --> 00:24:08.550
How did you come to play Moe's?

458
00:24:08.650 --> 00:24:09.010
Yeah.

459
00:24:09.230 --> 00:24:11.210
Something we understand you hated.

460
00:24:11.470 --> 00:24:14.550
You hated being Moe's, but we love Moe's.

461
00:24:14.550 --> 00:24:18.430
Let me, let me, let me just delineate here importantly.

462
00:24:18.430 --> 00:24:21.770
I love the fact that I played Moe's.

463
00:24:22.250 --> 00:24:27.830
I hated actually doing it because, uh, for two reasons.

464
00:24:27.970 --> 00:24:29.150
So, so here's the story.

465
00:24:29.290 --> 00:24:35.690
The story was in the, in the, Greg always had it as part of Dwight Schrute's backstory that

466
00:24:35.690 --> 00:24:43.710
he, he himself was not Amish or, um, or Mennonite or anything like that, but he was sort of Amish

467
00:24:43.710 --> 00:24:45.530
adjacent that he had relatives.

468
00:24:45.530 --> 00:24:50.430
He was a Germanic, he was of Germanic descent living in Pennsylvania.

469
00:24:50.990 --> 00:24:54.990
That is, um, uh, that's, that alone is sort of Amish adjacent.

470
00:24:55.190 --> 00:25:01.230
So Greg always had it in the back of his head that there was a sort of cousin, a Schrute cousin

471
00:25:01.230 --> 00:25:07.030
farming community that was, uh, that was either Amish or something in the Amish family.

472
00:25:08.350 --> 00:25:15.270
