00:50:41.520 --> 00:50:46.500
But was also like, this handsome man is massaging my wife's breasts right now.

927
00:50:46.960 --> 00:50:51.680
And, um, and I remember they came up to me and said, we think this is really funny,

928
00:50:53.220 --> 00:50:57.740
but we imagine that it would be weird for you to have a total stranger that you met that day

929
00:50:57.740 --> 00:50:58.960
come and handle your breasts.

930
00:50:58.960 --> 00:51:01.860
And the joke only works if they're actually touching your breasts.

931
00:51:02.100 --> 00:51:06.900
So what if your fiance Lee played the role?

932
00:51:07.060 --> 00:51:11.600
And then it's someone who's already massaging your breasts, massaging your breasts.

933
00:51:11.660 --> 00:51:14.400
And I was like, well, he does massage them.

934
00:51:14.560 --> 00:51:15.400
So that is true.

935
00:51:16.260 --> 00:51:18.220
And so it was great.

936
00:51:19.600 --> 00:51:21.920
So then it was just weird for John.

937
00:51:22.240 --> 00:51:22.420
Yeah.

938
00:51:22.500 --> 00:51:26.640
Then it was just weird for everyone else because we were super comfortable.

939
00:51:27.620 --> 00:51:31.880
Well, I think that's, that's first of all, good for them for obviously bringing that up,

940
00:51:31.960 --> 00:51:36.260
but also like that there is an enormous leap of faith that you're taking as an actor when

941
00:51:36.260 --> 00:51:37.700
you sign onto a TV show, right?

942
00:51:37.820 --> 00:51:42.520
You're, you're, you're really like, you're using a very small amount of data, which is

943
00:51:42.520 --> 00:51:47.980
usually like a pilot script and maybe a meeting with the show creator or the show

944
00:51:47.980 --> 00:51:48.240
runner.

945
00:51:48.600 --> 00:51:55.380
And you're extrapolating over potentially 10 years and 200 episodes of like, am I putting

946
00:51:55.380 --> 00:51:57.280
my faith and trust in this person?

947
00:51:57.680 --> 00:52:02.360
And is it going to be rewarded or are there going to be constant battles and fights about

948
00:52:02.360 --> 00:52:06.180
creative directions and things that they ask me to do that?

949
00:52:06.340 --> 00:52:09.940
And, and the, and that this is part of why being an actor, I think is so difficult is you

950
00:52:09.940 --> 00:52:13.160
only, your only advocate is yourself.

951
00:52:13.160 --> 00:52:18.520
Like you don't have a, you know, you maybe have an agent or a manager who can have your

952
00:52:18.520 --> 00:52:24.420
back in those moments, but it's like this idea of like the quote difficult actor and

953
00:52:24.420 --> 00:52:32.820
quote, like, I think that is a, a, a horrible misnomer that has come from now 80 years of actors

954
00:52:32.820 --> 00:52:38.280
being, getting a new script every week and not having any idea what's in it.

955
00:52:38.280 --> 00:52:43.860
And those ideas coming from people who maybe haven't run a bunch of things by them and just

956
00:52:43.860 --> 00:52:46.240
being like, Oh man, now I got to do this this week.

957
00:52:46.340 --> 00:52:50.120
And the, and the range of how it can make an actor feel uncomfortable is enormous.

958
00:52:50.360 --> 00:52:55.020
It can be anything from like a strange man has to massage parts of your body that you might

959
00:52:55.020 --> 00:53:00.260
not want that person to massage all the way to just creative decisions about the character,

960
00:53:00.400 --> 00:53:04.340
the choices, the character makes things that the character does that you don't agree

961
00:53:04.340 --> 00:53:04.600
with.

962
00:53:04.780 --> 00:53:06.520
Like it's just really hard.

963
00:53:06.680 --> 00:53:08.180
It's a, it's a very difficult thing.

964
00:53:08.340 --> 00:53:10.640
And the chance for it to go sideways is enormous.

965
00:53:10.920 --> 00:53:17.380
So the more communication there is between writers and actors, the better to bring this

966
00:53:17.380 --> 00:53:18.060
full circle.

967
00:53:18.280 --> 00:53:23.700
That's another thing that I remember thinking I could attribute to SNL a little bit with

968
00:53:23.700 --> 00:53:26.400
Greg, because Greg worked there at SNL.

969
00:53:26.480 --> 00:53:29.120
Like everybody does everything writers act and actors, right.

970
00:53:29.120 --> 00:53:32.180
And it's very like community theater kind of feeling.

971
00:53:32.700 --> 00:53:37.220
And there's no division between actors and writers really at all.

972
00:53:37.340 --> 00:53:39.280
Like there, it's all one big hodgepodge.

973
00:53:39.540 --> 00:53:45.760
And that was Greg's whole theory was like, let's, let's break down these artificial barriers

974
00:53:45.760 --> 00:53:47.280
between the writers and the actors.

975
00:53:47.440 --> 00:53:51.380
That's why he wanted Mindy and BJ and Paul to act in the show.

976
00:53:51.440 --> 00:53:56.240
It's why he want, it's why he gave the, this season two finale where Jim and Pam, where

977
00:53:56.240 --> 00:54:00.080
Jim tells Pam that he likes her or is in love with her and that they kiss for the first time.

978
00:54:00.300 --> 00:54:01.160
Steve wrote that.

979
00:54:01.300 --> 00:54:02.300
He gave that to Steve.

980
00:54:02.480 --> 00:54:07.560
Like that's traditionally the kind of thing that like he would write or like the co-EP,

981
00:54:07.880 --> 00:54:09.940
a trusted writer would write that script.

982
00:54:10.260 --> 00:54:15.900
But Greg saw the big picture and he understood fundamentally that the more there was that we,

983
00:54:16.000 --> 00:54:20.140
he broke down the divisions between writers and actors, the healthier the organism of the

984
00:54:20.140 --> 00:54:20.580
show would be.

985
00:54:20.680 --> 00:54:21.460
And he was totally right.

986
00:54:22.120 --> 00:54:22.840
So true.

987
00:54:23.180 --> 00:54:25.760
I mean, Jenna and I have talked about that a lot on this podcaSt.

988
00:54:26.240 --> 00:54:32.020
Other shows that we have done since the office, I did a guest star on a show and the writer's

989
00:54:32.020 --> 00:54:35.440
room was in New York and we filmed in LA.

990
00:54:35.800 --> 00:54:39.320
And I thought, wow, they never meet the writers.

991
00:54:39.460 --> 00:54:41.100
They never interact with them at all.

992
00:54:41.800 --> 00:54:48.020
And it's just been such a interesting and wonderful perspective to look back at how fortunate

993
00:54:48.020 --> 00:54:53.320
we were to have that sort of creative collaboration as our biggest job in our life.

994
00:54:53.320 --> 00:55:00.920
I've bounced up to writers on sets at other jobs thinking that the way we had it on the

995
00:55:00.920 --> 00:55:02.020
office was normal.

996
00:55:02.200 --> 00:55:03.940
And they seem terrified to speak to me.

997
00:55:04.660 --> 00:55:07.300
They are terrified that I am talking to them.

998
00:55:07.440 --> 00:55:09.180
They are afraid to say anything.

999
00:55:09.480 --> 00:55:11.140
I'll be like, hey, what's up?

1000
00:55:11.240 --> 00:55:11.800
What do you think?

1001
00:55:12.520 --> 00:55:16.320
Like next week, what do you think is going to be like, how's this story going to play

1002
00:55:16.320 --> 00:55:16.540
out?

1003
00:55:16.540 --> 00:55:20.140
And they're like, I don't want to speak to you.

1004
00:55:20.220 --> 00:55:25.040
The first time I did a multicam after the office, because I'd never done a multicam, all

1005
00:55:25.040 --> 00:55:28.240
the writers were like huddled in a corner, you know, like we're filming.

1006
00:55:28.340 --> 00:55:29.920
It's like filming a one-act play.

1007
00:55:30.460 --> 00:55:33.300
And they're all in the corner because I knew this joke wasn't working.

1008
00:55:33.460 --> 00:55:34.880
And I had like a few ideas.

1009
00:55:35.000 --> 00:55:37.960
So I went up to the huddle and I was like, hey, guys, I got like two or three pitches.

1010
00:55:38.200 --> 00:55:39.100
And they were like, what?

1011
00:55:39.100 --> 00:55:40.600
It was like a record scratch.

1012
00:55:42.520 --> 00:55:43.520
Yeah, I know.

1013
00:55:43.680 --> 00:55:50.600
And again, Greg's ethos was, Greg's ethos in a nutshell is best idea wins.

1014
00:55:50.800 --> 00:55:54.340
And he doesn't care whether the best idea comes from a writer, an actor, a director, an

1015
00:55:54.340 --> 00:55:58.140
accountant, a random person on the street who happens to walk by at the right moment

1016
00:55:58.140 --> 00:55:58.980
and has a good pitch.

1017
00:55:59.140 --> 00:56:01.960
Like it was just, why would we not take the best idea?

1018
00:56:02.220 --> 00:56:07.360
And so that fostered that sense of like, everybody give us all your ideas.

1019
00:56:07.360 --> 00:56:10.320
Like, let's put all of the ideas into a pot and pick the best one.

1020
00:56:10.540 --> 00:56:13.960
And that is very frequently, sadly, not how it works on other shows.

1021
00:56:14.160 --> 00:56:18.760
And it's a shame because, you know, there were times when we would be battling about

1022
00:56:18.760 --> 00:56:23.540
something in the edit bay about an episode and he'd have two alts and he would say, which

1023
00:56:23.540 --> 00:56:24.360
one do you like?

1024
00:56:24.500 --> 00:56:26.920
And all of the writers would choose version A.

1025
00:56:28.080 --> 00:56:31.960
And he would go like, and then he would call in like the accounting department.

1026
00:56:32.220 --> 00:56:32.400
Yeah.

1027
00:56:32.740 --> 00:56:33.960
And he'd be like, which one do you like?

1028
00:56:34.100 --> 00:56:34.820
The real accounting.

1029
00:56:34.900 --> 00:56:35.720
The actual accountants.

1030
00:56:35.780 --> 00:56:36.000
Yeah.

1031
00:56:36.000 --> 00:56:36.360
Yes.

1032
00:56:36.540 --> 00:56:37.980
Not you and Oscar.

1033
00:56:38.380 --> 00:56:39.320
Not me and Brian and Oscar.

1034
00:56:40.700 --> 00:56:42.040
And he would go, which one do you like?

1035
00:56:42.100 --> 00:56:43.080
And they would say, we like B.

1036
00:56:43.140 --> 00:56:44.200
And he's like, yeah, I think B is better.

1037
00:56:44.400 --> 00:56:46.100
And it drove us crazy sometimes.

1038
00:56:46.640 --> 00:56:48.800
But he was like, you're not the audience.

1039
00:56:49.000 --> 00:56:50.720
You're professional comedy writers.

1040
00:56:50.920 --> 00:56:54.980
Like, of course, you're going to like this weird offbeat thing that we did.

1041
00:56:55.180 --> 00:56:56.840
You know what the best example of this is?

1042
00:56:58.640 --> 00:57:02.400
So Jim's proposal to Pam at the gas station, right?

1043
00:57:02.400 --> 00:57:02.740
Mm-hmm.

1044
00:57:02.980 --> 00:57:04.080
So three versions.

1045
00:57:04.280 --> 00:57:05.160
I don't know if you remember this, Jenna.

1046
00:57:05.340 --> 00:57:06.320
We had three versions.

1047
00:57:07.040 --> 00:57:10.120
One of them was the car pulls over.

1048
00:57:10.340 --> 00:57:15.720
The camera is across the street and is shooting super long lens.

1049
00:57:15.720 --> 00:57:20.260
And all you hear is traffic whizzing by in the foreground.

1050
00:57:20.980 --> 00:57:24.300
And you can't hear, they're not mic'd in this version.

1051
00:57:24.760 --> 00:57:25.560
The sound was off.

1052
00:57:25.560 --> 00:57:29.180
So you saw Jim walk up and you saw Pam and then, ha-ha.

1053
00:57:29.300 --> 00:57:30.620
And then all of a sudden, they're talking.

1054
00:57:30.760 --> 00:57:31.540
You don't know what they're saying.

1055
00:57:31.880 --> 00:57:33.000
Jim gets down on one knee.

1056
00:57:33.260 --> 00:57:34.180
Pam reacts.

1057
00:57:35.660 --> 00:57:37.980
And then they hug and they kiss and it's over.

1058
00:57:38.520 --> 00:57:44.120
And second version was, or the final version was like very traditionally shot.

1059
00:57:44.240 --> 00:57:49.380
Like you were right with them in the gas station, sort of looking in a classic TV coverage.

1060
00:57:49.520 --> 00:57:53.040
And you could still hear traffic whizzing by in the background, but you couldn't really see it.

1061
00:57:53.360 --> 00:57:54.800
And the cameras were right up close.

1062
00:57:55.560 --> 00:58:00.560
Sort of intermediate version where you were a little bit further away, but you could hear what they were saying.

1063
00:58:01.040 --> 00:58:02.920
And he screened all three of them.

1064
00:58:03.100 --> 00:58:12.720
And the one that was where the camera was super far away and you couldn't hear a word gave me like the greatest goosebump feeling of my life.

1065
00:58:12.840 --> 00:58:14.520
Like I was like, that's so beautiful.

1066
00:58:14.760 --> 00:58:16.180
It's so perfect for the show.

1067
00:58:16.500 --> 00:58:21.560
It reminded me of the scene in the British office where Tim takes off his mic and goes in and talks to Don.

1068
00:58:21.740 --> 00:58:24.480
That was the sort of model of it where, and you don't hear what they're saying.

1069
00:58:24.480 --> 00:58:27.620
And then he comes back out and grabs the mic and says, she said no, by the way.

1070
00:58:27.840 --> 00:58:30.300
And it's terrible and sad.

1071
00:58:31.640 --> 00:58:33.300
And I was like, well, that's a home run.

1072
00:58:33.500 --> 00:58:34.480
That's like the most beautiful.

1073
00:58:34.740 --> 00:58:35.420
It's so perfect.

1074
00:58:35.460 --> 00:58:36.580
It's the most beautiful thing ever.

1075
00:58:37.080 --> 00:58:38.660
And he was like, yeah, we're not using that version.

1076
00:58:38.960 --> 00:58:39.800
And I was like, why?

1077
00:58:39.800 --> 00:58:49.780
And he was like, because this is a moment that fans of the show have been waiting for, for five, six years, whatever it was.

1078
00:58:49.780 --> 00:58:58.480
And it's not, it's just fundamentally not fair to deny them the ability to hear Jim say, will you marry me?

1079
00:58:58.580 --> 00:58:59.360
It's just mean.

1080
00:58:59.540 --> 00:59:09.120
It's like, and, and this might be the most sort of like beautiful, artistic, you know, like highfalutin version.

1081
00:59:10.260 --> 00:59:17.200
But if, if, what, what are we doing if we're denying fans the ability to hear Jim say, will you marry me?

1082
00:59:17.320 --> 00:59:19.400
And hear Pam say, yes, that's just cruel.

1083
00:59:20.140 --> 00:59:22.920
And I always fought him on all of those decisions.

1084
00:59:23.080 --> 00:59:24.460
And he was always right.

1085
00:59:25.620 --> 00:59:29.860
Just like at the end of the day, I was like, of course, of course you need to hear, will you marry me?

1086
00:59:29.860 --> 00:59:35.000
Yes, like that, that's such a foundational moment in the history of the show.

1087
00:59:35.240 --> 00:59:42.140
And so he had that, he had that thing of like, let's develop and explore every possible idea.

1088
00:59:42.660 --> 00:59:44.320
Let's walk down every path.

1089
00:59:44.460 --> 00:59:46.720
Let's pull every name out of the hat.

1090
00:59:47.060 --> 00:59:51.820
And at the end of the day, we will choose the one that is the best for the show.

1091
00:59:51.980 --> 00:59:57.980
Not the one that's loved the most by the people who work on the show or the writers who write things or any, anything else.

1092
00:59:57.980 --> 01:00:00.500
Just what is the best thing for the show?

1093
01:00:00.760 --> 01:00:06.600
And that's why the show over the course of its decade long run had such an integrity to it, I think.

1094
01:00:06.760 --> 01:00:10.760
It's because it was always, the decisions were always made in terms of what's best for the show.

1095
01:00:10.940 --> 01:00:16.000
Not, not what a bunch of like jaded writers thought would be the coolest thing or something like that.

1096
01:00:17.640 --> 01:00:21.100
Do you want to ask Mike about the Christmas lights?

1097
01:00:21.500 --> 01:00:22.840
Oh yeah, I do.

1098
01:00:24.560 --> 01:00:26.160
This is such a hard pivot.

1099
01:00:26.940 --> 01:00:32.220
But we, okay, so in the first Christmas episode, which, oh my God, I just love that episode so much.

1100
01:00:32.300 --> 01:00:33.580
I can watch it like every year.

1101
01:00:33.720 --> 01:00:36.020
It's almost like, you know, what do you like to watch at the holidays?

1102
01:00:36.180 --> 01:00:38.680
Oh, that, you know, Christmas vacation with Chevy Chase.

1103
01:00:38.860 --> 01:00:41.360
I like to watch the very first Christmas episode.

1104
01:00:41.760 --> 01:00:42.160
The office Christmas Yankee Swap.

1105
01:00:42.260 --> 01:00:47.340
I have had a Yankee Swap party for years inspired by that episode.

1106
01:00:47.380 --> 01:00:48.840
I love everything about it.

1107
01:00:48.840 --> 01:01:01.780
So, there was this moment that we heard was inspired by your real life where when they put up the Christmas tree, the tiny, tiny Christmas lights and Angela's so mad at Phyllis.

1108
01:01:01.900 --> 01:01:02.940
She's so pissed off.

1109
01:01:03.120 --> 01:01:06.040
And we heard that was like actually based on something in your life.

1110
01:01:06.220 --> 01:01:07.840
Were there, was, is that true?

1111
01:01:07.980 --> 01:01:11.940
And were there other moments like that, like things that were in your life that made it into the show?

1112
01:01:11.940 --> 01:01:13.200
Totally true.

1113
01:01:13.580 --> 01:01:24.700
My first Christmas in LA, my, my then fiance, now wife, JJ, and I got our, our first tree as cohabitants.

1114
01:01:25.060 --> 01:01:26.860
And we were so excited.

1115
01:01:27.080 --> 01:01:28.220
We both love Christmas.

1116
01:01:28.220 --> 01:01:30.020
We love like decorating the tree.

1117
01:01:30.160 --> 01:01:30.740
We love everything.

1118
01:01:30.740 --> 01:01:33.260
And we bought the tree and we took it home.

1119
01:01:33.400 --> 01:01:40.040
We put it up and we played Christmas music and we had bought these, my, in my house growing up, you always did only white lights.

1120
01:01:40.200 --> 01:01:40.740
I don't know why.

1121
01:01:40.740 --> 01:01:43.140
That was the, that was like what our family preferred.

1122
01:01:43.940 --> 01:01:46.680
And JJ had been like, I like the big colored ones.

1123
01:01:46.820 --> 01:01:48.480
They're so like cheery and happy.

1124
01:01:49.060 --> 01:01:51.160
And I, for some reason, won the argument.

1125
01:01:51.320 --> 01:01:53.460
I don't know why, but I was like, trust me, it looks great.

1126
01:01:53.540 --> 01:01:54.020
It looks great.

1127
01:01:54.340 --> 01:02:01.680
And so, I went and bought those little tiny white lights and I very carefully strung them around the tree and everything.

1128
01:02:01.960 --> 01:02:05.880
And then we, we like, we're playing Christmas music and it was like a big countdown.

1129
01:02:06.000 --> 01:02:09.720
And I turned them on and you absolutely could not see any of them.

1130
01:02:09.720 --> 01:02:13.760
It was the saddest, the least, the leaSt.

1131
01:02:13.880 --> 01:02:17.160
It was the opposite of the Rockefeller Center Christmas tree lighting.

1132
01:02:18.240 --> 01:02:25.140
And, and I was immediately humiliated and I turned and my wife was just laughing at me so hard.

1133
01:02:25.360 --> 01:02:27.720
Like, just what a complete failure this was.

1134
01:02:27.900 --> 01:02:33.480
And I immediately went out and went to the drugstore and bought big, big colored lights and came home and we strung them up.

1135
01:02:33.480 --> 01:02:40.260
And so, I, but I remember that, that feeling and like that episode is so much about like expectation versus reality.

1136
01:02:40.560 --> 01:02:47.540
And so, I was like, oh, that would be a good, like little metaphor for what's about to happen to Michael Scott when he gets Phyllis' oven mitt.

1137
01:02:47.540 --> 01:02:57.340
So, I just, I put it in as like, Greg was so into, and the show was so into those tiny, tiny, tiny observable moments of real life.

1138
01:02:57.800 --> 01:03:00.240
And, and so I was like, well, that makes perfect sense.

1139
01:03:00.340 --> 01:03:05.580
And my favorite thing about that is after they turn the lights on and Michael goes, not great.

1140
01:03:05.700 --> 01:03:10.520
There's a quick pan to Angela who just looks angrier than she has ever looked in her entire life.

1141
01:03:10.520 --> 01:03:16.720
I know, that episode gave me a full headache because I was just always so angry.

1142
01:03:16.960 --> 01:03:18.400
Scowling, just scowling with everybody.

1143
01:03:18.920 --> 01:03:28.420
Yeah, I mean, there, that, so much of what happened in those first, in that, especially in the first season and a half, you know, seasons one and two,

1144
01:03:28.720 --> 01:03:32.900
so much of that stuff was real observation, like about people's lives.

1145
01:03:32.900 --> 01:03:41.040
Like all of the gifts in that, that I invented for, the, this, this was another great writing lesson that came from that episode.

1146
01:03:41.260 --> 01:03:51.520
So, in my original version, the person who had Angela, maybe, no, Oscar had Creed, who had, who had, who had, who had, Toby, Toby, right.

1147
01:03:51.620 --> 01:03:51.780
Yeah.

1148
01:03:52.020 --> 01:03:52.200
Right.

1149
01:03:52.360 --> 01:03:58.220
So, Toby, in the original version, had a talking head where he was like, I don't know a lot about Angela.

1150
01:03:58.220 --> 01:04:09.100
I know that she's religious, so I bought her this book, and the book was one of those sort of religious adjacent self-help books, you know, like sort of like leading,

1151
01:04:09.120 --> 01:04:11.840
leading a spiritual life or something.

1152
01:04:12.060 --> 01:04:13.360
It's one of those kind of hokey books.

1153
01:04:14.640 --> 01:04:25.260
And Greg was like, I don't think that he should give her that book because we already know that Angela has that tendency or leans in that direction.

1154
01:04:25.260 --> 01:04:27.820
And so, you're not, the audience isn't getting any new information.

1155
01:04:29.000 --> 01:04:31.920
And I was like, all right, well, like what could it be?

1156
01:04:32.520 --> 01:04:39.320
And then someone, it might have been Greg, pitched that thing of a poster of babies who are dressed like adults.

1157
01:04:39.560 --> 01:04:39.680
Yeah.

1158
01:04:39.920 --> 01:04:43.460
And we, nobody could understand why, but it was the perfect thing.

1159
01:04:43.600 --> 01:04:48.080
It was just, that's a perfect thing that it was like, you're learning more about Angela, right?

1160
01:04:48.080 --> 01:04:54.240
You've got a new piece of information about her character, but it's still very much in keeping with what we already know about her.

1161
01:04:54.240 --> 01:04:54.860
Right, right.

1162
01:04:55.040 --> 01:05:00.220
And then that, and then that poster became like basis of like four other stories over the course of the show.

1163
01:05:00.220 --> 01:05:04.660
Oh yeah, it became a huge source of contention between Angela and Oscar because she hung it by her desk.

1164
01:05:04.940 --> 01:05:07.160
And then Oscar had to wear it as a, as a shirt.

1165
01:05:07.280 --> 01:05:07.520
Yeah.

1166
01:05:08.900 --> 01:05:15.580
Well, Angela in real life really likes animals who are dressed in costumes of other animals.

1167
01:05:15.920 --> 01:05:16.280
I mean.

1168
01:05:16.360 --> 01:05:17.460
It's very similar.

1169
01:05:17.660 --> 01:05:20.840
It's very similar to liking babies dressed as adults, I think.

1170
01:05:20.840 --> 01:05:25.840
I mean, animals dressed as any, anything really like if, if on Halloween you dress your dog

1171
01:05:25.840 --> 01:05:29.960
as a hot dog, I'm going to be like, Oh my God, your dog's a hot dog.

1172
01:05:30.300 --> 01:05:32.960
So we have two dogs and this year they were ketchup and mustard.

1173
01:05:33.060 --> 01:05:34.220
Oh, I love it.

1174
01:05:34.340 --> 01:05:36.300
Oh, I would love to see a picture of that.

1175
01:05:36.320 --> 01:05:37.940
That lights up Angela's life.

1176
01:05:38.240 --> 01:05:39.480
It really does.

1177
01:05:39.500 --> 01:05:43.440
So wait, animals dressed like other animals, you mean like, like a, like a dog dressed like

1178
01:05:43.440 --> 01:05:44.360
a cow or like.

1179
01:05:44.560 --> 01:05:44.880
Oh yeah.

1180
01:05:44.880 --> 01:05:47.960
Or like a teddy bear that's, but it has like a bunny hat.

1181
01:05:49.040 --> 01:05:49.520
Yeah.

1182
01:05:49.640 --> 01:05:49.960
Fantastic.

1183
01:05:50.060 --> 01:05:50.340
I know.

1184
01:05:50.520 --> 01:05:50.720
Why?

1185
01:05:50.880 --> 01:05:51.700
I don't know why.

1186
01:05:55.540 --> 01:05:59.040
Mike, thank you so much for coming on Office Ladies today.

1187
01:05:59.140 --> 01:06:00.080
This was such a treat.

1188
01:06:00.240 --> 01:06:03.560
We want to tell people that you wrote a book.

1189
01:06:04.020 --> 01:06:04.500
Yes.

1190
01:06:04.640 --> 01:06:06.640
And the book is called How to Be Perfect.

1191
01:06:06.820 --> 01:06:09.560
It comes out on January 25th.

1192
01:06:10.040 --> 01:06:11.160
Tell us about it.

1193
01:06:11.620 --> 01:06:18.540
So this book came out of the show, The Good Place that I created, which was about, if you

1194
01:06:18.540 --> 01:06:22.200
haven't seen it, it's very hard to explain it in one sentence, but it's essentially a,

1195
01:06:22.380 --> 01:06:25.600
I pitched it as a show about like what it means to be a good person.

1196
01:06:25.820 --> 01:06:34.340
And for the show, I read a lot of ethics and philosophy by a lot of very old, boring people.

1197
01:06:34.340 --> 01:06:39.900
And I thought, I had this thought while I was researching it, which was that these people

1198
01:06:39.900 --> 01:06:42.580
have such good ideas for how to be better people.

1199
01:06:42.780 --> 01:06:46.920
Like it's all of these ethical theories and this moral philosophy is like, it's like a

1200
01:06:46.920 --> 01:06:52.320
how-to guide for like living a good life and being a good person on earth, but their writing

1201
01:06:52.320 --> 01:06:55.180
is so tortured and boring that no one wants to read it.

1202
01:06:55.320 --> 01:07:00.480
So maybe I could write a version where I sort of try to explain as best as I can what their

1203
01:07:00.480 --> 01:07:04.000
theories are, but in a way that doesn't give you a tension headache.

1204
01:07:04.000 --> 01:07:05.380
And make you sleepy.

1205
01:07:05.700 --> 01:07:06.900
So that's what it is.

1206
01:07:07.040 --> 01:07:10.800
It's sort of a summary of a lot of different theories and moral philosophy.

1207
01:07:11.080 --> 01:07:16.320
The way that the book is sort of organized is like, I pose a bunch of boring questions

1208
01:07:16.320 --> 01:07:19.720
or mundane questions that face us every day.

1209
01:07:19.820 --> 01:07:23.780
Like if my friend bought an ugly shirt and says, what do you think of my shirt?

1210
01:07:23.880 --> 01:07:25.340
Is it okay to lie to her?

1211
01:07:25.480 --> 01:07:29.340
Or do you have to tell the truth?

1212
01:07:29.560 --> 01:07:32.960
And so it's questions like that, that are just things that come up in everyday life.

1213
01:07:32.960 --> 01:07:37.820
And then I try to explain what like different moral philosophers and ethicists would say

1214
01:07:37.820 --> 01:07:38.620
about those questions.

1215
01:07:39.500 --> 01:07:40.680
So that's the idea.

1216
01:07:40.860 --> 01:07:41.980
And it's on sale January.

1217
01:07:42.180 --> 01:07:42.860
It's on sale now.

1218
01:07:43.060 --> 01:07:46.060
You can buy it now from any online bookstore that you prefer.

1219
01:07:47.200 --> 01:07:50.840
And also all of the proceeds are going to charity.

1220
01:07:50.840 --> 01:07:56.400
So you can buy it and know that you're doing something good in the world by having money go

1221
01:07:56.400 --> 01:07:56.760
to charity.

1222
01:07:56.920 --> 01:07:57.960
Oh, I love that.

1223
01:07:57.960 --> 01:08:01.560
Just by buying the book, I am being a good person.

1224
01:08:01.800 --> 01:08:01.980
That's right.

1225
01:08:01.980 --> 01:08:02.380
That's right.

1226
01:08:02.480 --> 01:08:03.140
That's the idea.

1227
01:08:03.300 --> 01:08:03.380
Yeah.

1228
01:08:03.800 --> 01:08:06.720
So you're really setting me up to succeed.

1229
01:08:07.280 --> 01:08:08.200
I like it.

1230
01:08:08.280 --> 01:08:08.840
I like it too.

1231
01:08:09.400 --> 01:08:13.520
You know right away that you've made the world a slightly better place just by buying

1232
01:08:13.520 --> 01:08:13.920
the book.

1233
01:08:14.340 --> 01:08:15.340
It's that easy.

1234
01:08:15.460 --> 01:08:16.800
It's that easy to be a good person.

1235
01:08:16.960 --> 01:08:17.820
I love it.

1236
01:08:17.920 --> 01:08:20.460
Well, this is what Mindy Kaling had to say about your book.

1237
01:08:20.460 --> 01:08:26.720
She said, as someone who worries that a deep dive into morality will ruin my fun and problematic

1238
01:08:26.720 --> 01:08:29.640
life, I was certain this book wouldn't be for me.

1239
01:08:29.840 --> 01:08:31.260
Boy, was I wrong.

1240
01:08:31.440 --> 01:08:36.200
It's so brilliant and funny and warmly written, you don't realize you're becoming a better

1241
01:08:36.200 --> 01:08:37.780
person just by reading it.

1242
01:08:38.800 --> 01:08:39.320
Yes.

1243
01:08:39.400 --> 01:08:41.620
It was very nice of her to blurb the book.

1244
01:08:41.740 --> 01:08:45.120
Her blurb is actually the one that made the cover, which I don't think I've told it yet.

1245
01:08:45.120 --> 01:08:45.740
Cover blurb.

1246
01:08:45.800 --> 01:08:46.900
Oh, cover blurb.

1247
01:08:46.900 --> 01:08:47.880
I found it online.

1248
01:08:47.900 --> 01:08:49.680
That's fancy blurb right there.

1249
01:08:50.480 --> 01:08:52.780
Well, I'm so excited to read it.

1250
01:08:53.020 --> 01:08:57.980
Yes, I have ordered my copy and Angela has two and we're going to get them.

1251
01:08:58.280 --> 01:09:05.100
And Mike, thank you because I, well, I'm a huge fan of your Twitter as well.

1252
01:09:05.360 --> 01:09:15.980
I feel like you're very, very good at taking complex ideas or thoughts and making them really

1253
01:09:15.980 --> 01:09:17.760
digestible and entertaining.

1254
01:09:17.760 --> 01:09:21.780
Meaning, you're also very good at breaking down baseball statistics.

1255
01:09:22.440 --> 01:09:22.700
Yes.

1256
01:09:23.080 --> 01:09:28.040
I have found, I go to your Twitter if there's a big game on because I'm like, oh, well,

1257
01:09:28.200 --> 01:09:31.660
I know that Mike is going to explain this to me because I don't know what all the hullabaloo's

1258
01:09:31.660 --> 01:09:31.920
about.

1259
01:09:33.360 --> 01:09:34.680
That's very kind of you to say.

1260
01:09:34.680 --> 01:09:43.680
I often wonder who in the world is still reading my Twitter because it is either in ridiculous arcane

1261
01:09:43.680 --> 01:09:49.460
sports data or just screaming about something that a politician said.

1262
01:09:49.660 --> 01:09:55.380
And it's like the Venn diagram for the two groups of people that want both of those things has to be

1263
01:09:55.380 --> 01:09:55.820
tiny.

1264
01:09:55.840 --> 01:10:02.160
So I'm glad to know that you two are still in the Venn diagram overlap of people who read my Twitter.

1265
01:10:02.160 --> 01:10:02.880
I am.

1266
01:10:03.020 --> 01:10:05.020
I don't understand your sports analysis.

1267
01:10:05.180 --> 01:10:05.960
Not always, no.

1268
01:10:06.100 --> 01:10:11.760
But I find it just sort of fascinating to watch your brain work.

1269
01:10:11.900 --> 01:10:12.240
Yes.

1270
01:10:12.400 --> 01:10:13.520
And we love data.

1271
01:10:13.700 --> 01:10:18.000
Jenna and I have discovered one of the things we have in common is we love tracking things.

1272
01:10:18.020 --> 01:10:19.440
We get very excited.

1273
01:10:19.660 --> 01:10:22.080
Just you name it, we track it on the show.

1274
01:10:22.220 --> 01:10:26.800
Like I became obsessed about every plant that was at front reception.

1275
01:10:26.800 --> 01:10:29.820
And I would document how every week there was a new plant.

1276
01:10:30.140 --> 01:10:35.520
And that's something that I find fascinating about baseball is there's so many different stats.

1277
01:10:35.940 --> 01:10:40.280
I don't understand them, but I will go to your Twitter when there's a big game and people are

1278
01:10:40.280 --> 01:10:41.360
talking about stats.

1279
01:10:41.560 --> 01:10:41.820
Yes.

1280
01:10:42.080 --> 01:10:42.240
Yeah.

1281
01:10:42.520 --> 01:10:43.000
Pitches.

1282
01:10:43.080 --> 01:10:44.540
You really break down the pitches.

1283
01:10:45.240 --> 01:10:45.520
Yeah.

1284
01:10:45.520 --> 01:10:51.120
Well, part of what some people love about baseball and what some people hate about it is the amount

1285
01:10:51.120 --> 01:10:52.400
of data that you can analyze.

1286
01:10:52.400 --> 01:10:54.100
I love it because I'm the same way.

1287
01:10:54.200 --> 01:10:58.100
I get nerdy about numbers and stats and stuff.

1288
01:10:58.480 --> 01:11:04.840
But again, when I go to my Twitter account and post 11 consecutive tweets about whether

1289
01:11:04.840 --> 01:11:09.920
a certain pitch was a ball or a strike, I do have the thought of like, who is this for?

1290
01:11:09.920 --> 01:11:14.160
Like, who is reading this right now and enjoying it?

1291
01:11:14.280 --> 01:11:17.380
But that's the joy of Twitter is you don't ever have to know if you don't want to know.

1292
01:11:17.540 --> 01:11:19.160
You can just send it out to the university.

1293
01:11:19.560 --> 01:11:20.520
And who cares?

1294
01:11:21.300 --> 01:11:23.680
Well, we have one final wrap-up question for you.

1295
01:11:23.840 --> 01:11:29.480
We always ask everyone who is on the show or worked on the show if they took anything from the set.

1296
01:11:30.740 --> 01:11:31.180
Okay.

1297
01:11:31.420 --> 01:11:37.620
So unlike probably many of the people that you've interviewed, I left in the middle.

1298
01:11:37.880 --> 01:11:38.060
Right.

1299
01:11:38.060 --> 01:11:42.980
I was around for the first four seasons and then the beginning of season five.

1300
01:11:43.280 --> 01:11:45.800
And then Greg and I started developing Parks and Recreation together.

1301
01:11:45.800 --> 01:11:48.000
I worked out of the offices.

1302
01:11:48.340 --> 01:11:49.400
Like, I was still around.

1303
01:11:50.320 --> 01:11:54.940
But slowly over the course of season five, I receded into the background.

1304
01:11:55.520 --> 01:12:02.380
So I didn't know, I think, sort of intellectually understand that I was leaving, leaving.

1305
01:12:03.140 --> 01:12:11.540
And so I didn't really think to grab anything specific from the set because it was like, it was still being used.

1306
01:12:11.700 --> 01:12:11.880
Right.

1307
01:12:12.140 --> 01:12:13.380
We're still making the show.

1308
01:12:13.380 --> 01:12:15.800
So I really didn't take anything.

1309
01:12:15.960 --> 01:12:20.420
I have a couple things that I do treasure that are related to the show.

1310
01:12:20.420 --> 01:12:28.520
One of which was, when I left, Lee Eisenberg and Gene Stupnitzky went to Phil Shea, Property Master Phil Shea,

1311
01:12:28.600 --> 01:12:36.500
and got his contact for who made the Dwight bobblehead that Angela had given to Dwight as a present.

1312
01:12:36.500 --> 01:12:42.280
And they had a Mike bobblehead made as like, it was sort of like a parting gift.

1313
01:12:42.300 --> 01:12:44.240
And so I still have the Mike bobblehead.

1314
01:12:44.340 --> 01:12:45.920
The only problem is I used to wear glasses.

1315
01:12:46.040 --> 01:12:47.100
I wear contacts now every day.

1316
01:12:47.200 --> 01:12:51.960
But back then I wore glasses pretty often because we would often work until like, you know, one in the morning.

1317
01:12:52.780 --> 01:12:54.240
And my contacts would dry out.

1318
01:12:54.360 --> 01:12:57.760
And the Mike bobblehead had actual glasses on.

1319
01:12:57.900 --> 01:13:01.680
And at some point, I think when we moved, the glasses broke off, which is a bummer.

1320
01:13:01.680 --> 01:13:05.700
And I've often thought about trying to repair them somehow and put them back on.

1321
01:13:05.900 --> 01:13:07.440
So I have a Mike bobblehead.

1322
01:13:07.680 --> 01:13:10.420
And I do have somewhere, and I don't even know where,

1323
01:13:10.980 --> 01:13:21.060
there was a moment at which everyone realized that the world's best boss mug would be a thing that was going to matter for the run of the show.

1324
01:13:21.240 --> 01:13:27.780
And Phil ordered like 24 of them just as in case they broke or in case we needed more of them or whatever.

1325
01:13:28.340 --> 01:13:31.460
And I asked him if I could have one, and he gave me one.

1326
01:13:31.460 --> 01:13:34.700
So I have a world's best boss mug, not one that was ever used.

1327
01:13:34.800 --> 01:13:35.720
It's not a famous one.

1328
01:13:35.840 --> 01:13:37.620
It's not the one from the opening credits or anything.

1329
01:13:37.800 --> 01:13:43.720
But I do have a world's best boss mug that is technically speaking from the set, I guess.

1330
01:13:43.860 --> 01:13:46.640
If you want to stretch the definition of from the set.

1331
01:13:46.780 --> 01:13:49.980
And you know what else you have is you have your binder with all your notes.

1332
01:13:50.120 --> 01:13:51.520
That is amazing to me.

1333
01:13:51.640 --> 01:13:52.260
I have that.

1334
01:13:52.460 --> 01:13:55.920
I have a lot of notebooks that I still look at from those early days.

1335
01:13:56.640 --> 01:13:57.040
Yeah.

1336
01:13:57.040 --> 01:13:59.740
And I'm sure there's a couple other things here and there somewhere.

1337
01:14:01.200 --> 01:14:06.840
Someone, I think after the finale, Wardrobe asked me if I wanted to keep any of the Moe's clothes.

1338
01:14:07.480 --> 01:14:10.500
And I believe what I said was, please burn them in a fire.

1339
01:14:14.240 --> 01:14:15.400
Oh my gosh, Mike.

1340
01:14:15.520 --> 01:14:16.860
This was so fun.

1341
01:14:17.040 --> 01:14:18.200
Thank you so much.

1342
01:14:18.400 --> 01:14:19.020
Thank you so much.

1343
01:14:19.020 --> 01:14:19.460
Thank you for having me.

1344
01:14:19.700 --> 01:14:20.880
It's so nice to see you too.

1345
01:14:20.960 --> 01:14:24.380
I'm so pleased that you are the official voice of the show.

1346
01:14:24.480 --> 01:14:28.160
It seems very fitting to me that the world is seeing the show through your eyes.

1347
01:14:28.340 --> 01:14:28.700
Oh.

1348
01:14:29.000 --> 01:14:30.480
That's very kind of you to say.

1349
01:14:30.680 --> 01:14:31.380
We're loving it.

1350
01:14:31.520 --> 01:14:35.940
It was amazing.

1351
01:14:36.540 --> 01:14:37.760
I love talking to Mike.

1352
01:14:37.840 --> 01:14:38.600
I could talk to him forever.

1353
01:14:38.880 --> 01:14:39.380
Me too.

1354
01:14:39.380 --> 01:14:44.080
And he said to us, you guys, he was like, if you ever have any questions for me about anything

1355
01:14:44.080 --> 01:14:46.420
for the office or other things, let me know.

1356
01:14:46.540 --> 01:14:48.760
And I was like, Jenna, how often can we text him?

1357
01:14:50.000 --> 01:14:50.440
Exactly.

1358
01:14:50.740 --> 01:14:51.080
Exactly.

1359
01:14:51.840 --> 01:14:52.500
Yes, Mike.

1360
01:14:52.660 --> 01:14:54.680
Get ready for an email flurry.

1361
01:14:55.100 --> 01:14:56.460
You love the word flurry.

1362
01:14:57.120 --> 01:14:58.000
It's cute.

1363
01:14:58.140 --> 01:14:59.240
It's a flurry.

1364
01:14:59.640 --> 01:15:03.360
I mean, instead of Jenna's blowing up your phone, she flurries up your phone.

1365
01:15:03.580 --> 01:15:04.360
I'm going to flurry it.

1366
01:15:05.080 --> 01:15:09.460
Well, you guys, Mike's book is called How to Be Perfect, The Correct Answer to Every

1367
01:15:09.460 --> 01:15:10.200
Moral Question.

1368
01:15:10.200 --> 01:15:11.580
It is out now.

1369
01:15:11.860 --> 01:15:17.640
And Mike really is giving away 100% of everything he's making on his book to five different

1370
01:15:17.640 --> 01:15:19.740
charities, and the book is awesome.

1371
01:15:20.100 --> 01:15:21.940
Well, that's our show this week, guys.

1372
01:15:22.100 --> 01:15:24.880
Thank you so much for listening to this episode of Office Ladies.

1373
01:15:25.260 --> 01:15:27.180
Next week, we have the promotion.

1374
01:15:27.720 --> 01:15:27.980
Yeah.

1375
01:15:28.480 --> 01:15:30.700
Jim gets a little mini office in the office.

1376
01:15:30.980 --> 01:15:32.220
He gets a glass box.

1377
01:15:32.220 --> 01:15:32.740
He does.

1378
01:15:33.040 --> 01:15:34.180
In the middle of the bullpen.

1379
01:15:34.360 --> 01:15:38.100
It's strange and interesting, and we'll tell you all about it.

1380
01:15:38.220 --> 01:15:39.120
See you next week.

1381
01:15:44.640 --> 01:15:46.400
Thank you for listening to Office Ladies.

